          <section id="overview">
            <h2>Overview</h2>
            <p>Bail plays an important role in balancing an accused person's right to liberty with the need to protect public safety and ensure attendance in court. The law classifies offences into bailable, non-bailable, and unbailable categories, each carrying different levels of judicial discretion and legal consequences. Through statutory provisions and judicial decisions, Malaysian courts continuously navigate the tension between the presumption of innocence and the interests of justice. This topic examines how bail jurisprudence operates within Malaysia's legal framework, particularly in cases involving serious offences and national security concerns.</p>
            <p>Bail is not merely a procedural mechanism for release. It is the vital constitutional bridge between the state's imperative to maintain security and the individual's fundamental right to liberty. In the Malaysian justice system, bail allows an unconvicted person to be released from custody whilst awaiting trial, ensuring their attendance in court through the provision of a security bond. At its core, the jurisprudence of bail is derived from the presumption of innocence, which is the universal legal doctrine that a person is presumed innocent until proven guilty in a court of law.</p>
            <p>The Malaysian Bar has long voiced out this principle, vigorously asserting that the denial of bail fundamentally undermines this presumption and emphasising that an unconvicted person's right to liberty and a fair trial must be zealously protected under the protective framework of the Federal Constitution. Consequently, the criminal justice system must never be permitted to devolve into a channel for inflicting "pre-trial punishment," which elevates the right to be considered for pre-trial release to a matter of profound constitutional significance.</p>
            <p>The primary statutory framework governing bail is codified within the Criminal Procedure Code (CPC), which categorises offences to carefully calibrate the scales of justice. While the general provisions of the CPC initially established a binary of bailable and non-bailable categories, the framework has evolved into a rigid three-tier system through the legislative addition of strictly unbailable offences.</p>
          </section>

          <section id="bailable">
            <h2>Bailable Offences <span class="section-ref">CPC s.387</span></h2>
            <p>Under Section 387 of the CPC, individuals accused of bailable offences possess an absolute, non-discretionary right to be released, and the authorities possess no statutory power to revoke this right. The absolute nature of this unimpeded liberty strictly prohibits the judicial imposition of restrictive conditions. In <em>Nicholas Hui Chung Weng v. Public Prosecutor</em>, the accused faced a bailable drug charge under Section 15(1)(a) of the Dangerous Drugs Act 1952 (DDA). Although the Magistrate granted bail, it erroneously attached restrictive conditions requiring the accused to surrender his international passport and report to the police station weekly. Upon revision, the High Court decisively annulled these conditions, holding that where an accused is entitled to bail as of right, the court is completely denuded of judicial discretion to refuse it, and consequently, the question of imposing restrictive conditions can never arise.</p>
          </section>

          <section id="non-bailable">
            <h2>Non-Bailable Offences <span class="section-ref">CPC s.388</span></h2>
            <p>Conversely, for non-bailable offences governed by Section 388 of the CPC, bail transitions from an absolute right into a meticulously weighed privilege. The power to grant bail in these severe instances falls entirely within the inherent judicial discretion of the courts. This discretion tracks its modern lineage to the landmark case of <em>Sulaiman bin Kadir v. Public Prosecutor</em>, which established that granting bail for severe non-bailable offences requires the strict proof of "exceptional and special reasons". Presiding judicial officers are duty-bound to evaluate critical risk factors, including the gravity of the offence, the likelihood of the accused absconding, and the potential for witness tampering or the commission of repeated offences.</p>
            <p>Because release within the non-bailable tier operates as a conditional privilege, courts are fully empowered to attach stringent conditions to secure the accused's attendance. In <em>Public Prosecutor v. Dato' Mat Safuan</em>, the Supreme Court established that while conditions such as the surrender of an international passport are entirely lawful for non-bailable offences, the High Court firmly retains supervisory jurisdiction to intervene and vary such conditions if they become unjust or unduly oppressive. Furthermore, because bail operates as a conditional privilege for non-bailable offences, the court retains the power to revoke it under Section 388(5) of the CPC. However, as established in <em>Phang Yong Fook v. Public Prosecutor</em>, the revocation of bail cannot be done arbitrarily. It demands concrete evidence, proven on a preponderance of probabilities, demonstrating witness tampering or active interference with the course of justice. It cannot be summarily revoked on vague, unsubstantiated allegations by the prosecution without affording the accused the right to be heard.</p>
            <p>Even within this strict discretionary category, the CPC incorporates a compassionate proviso, granting courts the explicit discretion to allow bail for any person under the age of sixteen years, any woman, or any sick or infirm person. Modern jurisprudence has simultaneously expanded to embrace technological alternatives to the absolute deprivation of liberty. Pursuant to Section 388A of the CPC, courts now possess the statutory power to attach an electronic monitoring device (EMD) to an accused person. This mechanism allows individuals to serve their pre-trial period within the community, mitigating flight risks while concurrently upholding fundamental liberties and alleviating prison overcrowding.</p>
            <p>Once the court exercises its discretion to grant release, Sections 389 to 393 of the CPC govern the execution of the bail mechanism. To ensure that bail does not morph into an oppressive, overwhelming financial barrier, Section 389 of the CPC strictly mandates that the quantum of bail must never be excessive. Concurrently, to guarantee that the accused remains legally tethered to the justice system, Sections 390, 392, and 393 of the CPC mandate the formal execution of a bail bond by the accused and their sureties, explicitly delineating the procedures for their discharge to ensure the accused remains legally bound to attend trial.</p>
          </section>

          <section id="unbailable">
            <h2>Unbailable Offences</h2>
            <p>While non-bailable offences allow the judiciary to balance pre-trial release as a discretionary privilege, strictly unbailable offences operate as an unyielding legislative mandate dictating that bail must be refused completely and absolutely. This absolute prohibition is typically dictated by specific, overriding penal statutes, such as Section 41B of the DDA, which unequivocally strips courts of their discretionary power for capital drug offences, as observed in <em>Loy Chin Hei v. Public Prosecutor</em>. This rigid statutory bar has triggered intense jurisprudential conflict regarding enhanced punishments and statutory exceptions.</p>
            <p>In <em>Murugan P Subramaniam v. Public Prosecutor</em>, the court attempted to pierce this legislative veil. There, the accused was charged under Section 15(1)(a) of the DDA, a bailable offence, but faced an enhanced punishment framework under Section 39C of the same Act due to prior drug convictions. The court originally granted bail, reasoning that Section 41B of the DDA did not explicitly listed Section 15(1)(a) within its restrictive scope. However, recent binding jurisprudence has decisively shut down this liberal interpretation, enforcing strict adherence to parliamentary intent.</p>
            <p>In <em>Lim Liah Wee v. Public Prosecutor</em>, the High Court hit a "brick wall" when refusing bail for a Section 39C DDA charge, firmly affirming that Section 41B of the DDA renders the offence absolutely unbailable. This conservative approach was unequivocally cemented in the recent case of <em>Yong Wai Kee v. Public Prosecutor</em>. The High Court ruled that because a charge under Section 39C of the DDA carries a mandatory minimum of five years' imprisonment, the statutory bar under Section 41B(1)(b) of the DDA applies automatically, completely ousting judicial discretion and rendering the offence unbailable.</p>
          </section>

          <section id="sosma">
            <h2>SOSMA <span class="section-ref">Security Offences (Special Measures) Act 2012</span></h2>
            <p>These procedural safeguards of the CPC are completely displaced by the special framework of the Security Offences (Special Measures) Act 2012 (SOSMA). Enacted under the broad emergency powers of Article 149 of the Federal Constitution (FC) to combat subversion and organised violence, SOSMA introduces profound procedural derogations from constitutional norms. On the surface, Section 13(1) of SOSMA enforces a categorical prohibition on bail for security offences. The Malaysian Bar, via a resolute 2024 resolution, unequivocally condemned this framework as oppressive and fundamentally contradictory to the rule of law. Drawing historic parallels to boycotts of draconian security laws, the Malaysian Bar argued that Section 13 of SOSMA effectively "steals" the inherent right of judges to consider bail, inflicting prolonged incarceration on individuals who have yet to be proven guilty and transforming the legal process into an instrument of premature punishment.</p>
            <p>Nonetheless, the appellate judiciary has stepped in to nuance this perceived absolute bar. Through the landmark rulings in <em>Tamil Selven a/l Letchumenan v. Public Prosecutor</em> and <em>Raman Shunmugham v. PP</em>, the courts have affirmed that Section 13(1) of SOSMA is not a total, unyielding bar to bail. The appellate courts equated the operational mechanics of Section 13 of SOSMA to Section 388 of the CPC, holding that security offences are properly categorised as "non-bailable" offences rather than absolutely "unbailable" ones, thereby preserving a narrow scope for judicial discretion via statutory exceptions.</p>
            <p>In the recent case of <em>Logan Segaran v. Kerajaan Malaysia</em>, the court held that this statutory restriction does not displace the constitutional presumption of innocence, as the ultimate guilt or innocence of the accused remains undetermined. Rather, bail is generally withheld due to the inherent gravity of security offences and the compelling state imperative to prevent flight risks, the destruction of evidence, and the intimidation of witnesses. The constitutional validity of this overarching framework is firmly protected by Article 149(1) of the FC, which expressly provides that security laws are valid notwithstanding any operational discrepancies with the fundamental liberties guaranteed under Article 5 of the FC.</p>
            <p>Even so, the courts have drawn strict boundaries around SOSMA's ambit to prevent executive overreach. In <em>Nathan Rajandran v. Public Prosecutor</em>, the Court of Appeal clarified that even if an offence is listed within SOSMA's First Schedule, it may not fall under the procedural restrictions of SOSMA if its true substance relates to Article 149(1)(e) of the FC. In such instances, Section 13 of SOSMA becomes inapplicable, and the right to apply for bail automatically reverts to Section 388 of the CPC.</p>
            <p>This strict restriction on pre-trial bail has triggered a fiercely contested jurisprudential battle over the separation of powers. In <em>Saminathan Ganesan v. Public Prosecutor</em>, the High Court delivered a landmark reproach, declaring Section 13 of SOSMA unconstitutional on the basis that the power to grant or refuse bail is an un-extinguishable, inherent judicial function protected by Article 121(1) of the FC. However, subsequent appellate rulings strictly reversed this stance to uphold the statute's validity. In <em>Md Nasir Uddin v. Public Prosecutor</em>, the court reaffirmed that SOSMA, as a specific security law enacted under the auspices of Article 149 of the FC, constitutionally prevails over the general bail provisions of the CPC. As reinforced by the apex court in <em>Datuk Seri Anwar Ibrahim v. Kerajaan Malaysia</em>, legislation shielded by the protective umbrella of Article 149 of the FC is legally tenable despite direct derogations from fundamental liberties, a stance which was affirmed in <em>Logan Segaran</em> to render the severe derogation of pre-trial liberty constitutionally sound.</p>
            <p>Crucially, however, the court in <em>Logan Segaran</em> struck down Section 30 of SOSMA, which mandated the continued detention of an accused person even after an acquittal by the court, ruling that such an extreme mandate violates Article 121(1) of the FC. By absolutely ousting judicial discretion post-acquittal, the court ruled that Parliament had committed a blatant legislative usurpation of judicial power, effectively violating the constitutionally entrenched separation of powers and reducing the judiciary to a mere "rubber stamp" of the executive branch.</p>
            <p>The rigidity of SOSMA's bail prohibition is only softened by Section 13(2) of the Act, which provides narrow exceptions for persons below the age of eighteen years, women, and sick or infirm persons. When the judiciary exercises its discretion under these highly restrictive exceptions, it imposes exceptionally stringent conditions to safeguard public interest and state security. In <em>Raman Shunmugham</em>, upon allowing bail for an infirm applicant, the court deemed it necessary to impose rigorous terms, setting bail at RM25,000 with two local sureties, requiring the immediate surrender of all travel documents, mandating the accused to report to the nearest police station monthly, and ordering the attachment of an electronic monitoring device (EMD) to the accused's leg.</p>
            <p>However, this statutory exception has exposed profound constitutional gaps, particularly concerning the doctrine of equality. In <em>Public Prosecutor v. Entiran a/l Durasamy</em>, Justice Su Tiang Joo recognised that denying bail to men while permitting it for women constitutes unlawful gender discrimination under Article 8 of the FC, a fundamental liberty that is not immunised from review by the text of Article 149 of the FC. To save the statute from unconstitutionality and uphold the guarantee of equal protection, the court interpreted the statutory term "woman" to legally encompass "men".</p>
            <p>Nevertheless, this interpretation was definitively struck down by the Federal Court in <em>Samat Yamin v. Public Prosecutor</em>. The Federal Court superseded <em>Entiran</em>'s ruling, determining that the statutory gender distinction is entirely constitutionally valid. Applying the traditional "reasonable classification" test under Article 8 of the FC, the apex court held that the distinction is justified by empirical evidence demonstrating that women face a heightened vulnerability to abuse in detention and present a significantly lower flight risk compared to men. The Federal Court also firmly dismissed earlier, unreported instances of male bail, such as <em>Public Prosecutor v. Koh Chin Wah &amp; Anor</em>, as non-binding, firmly closing the door on bail for male accused persons under this gender exception.</p>
            <p>Simultaneously, the appellate courts have remained highly vigilant in preventing the abuse of these medical exceptions. In the Court of Appeal decisions of <em>Shearvin Rajoo John v. Public Prosecutor</em> and its consolidated appeals, the court strictly applied the heavy evidentiary burden established by the apex court. These judgments collectively affirm that standard or manageable health conditions, such as chronic heart ailments, slipped discs, or recovering physical injuries, fall drastically short of the high threshold required to trigger the Section 13(2)(c) SOSMA exception. To justify pre-trial release under SOSMA, an illness must be exceptionally severe, immediately life-threatening, and substantiated by conclusive medical evidence, ensuring that judicial compassion never compromises the security of the state.</p>
          </section>

          <section id="capital">
            <h2>Capital Offences — The Compassionate Exception</h2>
            <p>While SOSMA and Section 39C of the DDA represent heavily enforced restrictions, the jurisprudential handling of strictly unbailable capital offences under the Penal Code reveals anomalies that strike at the heart of legal consistency. Capital offences such as murder under Section 302 of the Penal Code are legally deemed as non-bailable, designed for imprisonment pending trial. However, a parallel trajectory of case law exposes a profound jurisprudential contrast.</p>
            <p>This can be traced back to the landmark High Court decision in <em>Public Prosecutor v. Dato' Balwant Singh</em>, where a legal counsel facing a capital murder charge was granted a RM500,000 bail due to advanced age and ill health. This decision cemented the legal reality that the compassionate provisos embedded within Section 388 of the CPC possess the reach to pierce even the absolute veil of capital charges. This principle was resurrected by the apex court in the highly cited case of <em>Samirah Muzaffar v. Public Prosecutor</em>, where the Federal Court ordered the release of the accused on a massive bail of RM500,000 for a capital murder charge, explicitly citing her status as a woman and mother.</p>
            <p>This trend was further highlighted in <em>Ng Hui Yee v. Public Prosecutor</em>, where an accused facing a capital murder charge was similarly granted bail under the woman proviso. Furthermore, when the accused applied to reduce the excessive RM500,000 quantum, the High Court intervened to reduce it to RM100,000, ensuring her pre-trial release was not financially frustrated. This reveals an undeniable legal inconsistency in which, while the courts rely on strict statutory language to ruthlessly deny bail for drug-related and security offences, they also actively exercise their inherent jurisdiction to achieve humanitarian outcomes in capital offences governed by the CPC.</p>
          </section>

          <section id="international">
            <h2>International Comparison</h2>
            <p>To fully comprehend the severity of Malaysia's bail jurisprudence, a jurisdictional comparison is essential, revealing that the constitutional friction surrounding statutory bail prohibitions are issues faced by other jurisdictions too. In <em>Charles v. Attorney General of Trinidad and Tobago</em>, the Privy Council of the United Kingdom examined a statutory provision that entirely barred bail for murder, highlighting that the total removal of judicial discretion is a highly contentious constitutional manoeuver that severely strains the separation of powers. Similarly, in India, the Unlawful Activities (Prevention) Act 1967 (UAPA) heavily restricts pre-trial liberty, mandating that bail be denied if the accusations are prima facie true. However, in the breakthrough judgment of <em>Union of India v. K.A. Najeeb</em>, the Indian Supreme Court ruled that statutory restrictions cannot oust the inherent ability of constitutional courts to grant bail where there are unreasonable trial delays, relying on the constitutional right to a speedy trial.</p>
          </section>

          <section id="reform">
            <h2>Reform Recommendations</h2>
            <p>Conversely, under the Malaysian SOSMA regime, prolonged pre-trial incarceration is not currently recognised as a standalone, valid ground to pierce the statutory prohibition. This presents a highly potent, untapped jurisprudential avenue for Malaysia. While Article 149 of the FC protects SOSMA from being struck down under the fundamental liberties, prolonged pre-trial incarceration without trial directly violates the guarantee of right to life and liberty under Article 5(1) of the FC.</p>
            <p>As observed in <em>Public Prosecutor v. Dato' Mat Safuan</em>, where the Supreme Court explicitly recognised "an unduly delayed trial" as a compelling circumstance to revisit bail, the Malaysian courts must eventually adopt the Najeeb principle. Once pre-trial detention under SOSMA becomes excessively prolonged, the statutory bar on bail must yield to the overarching constitutional right to a speedy trial, as Article 149 of the FC does not immunise the state from transforming regulatory security detention into unauthorised pre-trial punishment.</p>
            <p>While the CPC attempts to meticulously balance justice through its bailable and non-bailable tiers, the emergence of unbailable mandates and the absolute statutory structures of SOSMA highlight a profound constitutional friction. As articulated by the former Chief Justice Tengku Maimun during the Opening of the Legal Year in 2020, the primary goal must be reshaping judicial reform to uphold public confidence. Preserving the presumption of innocence requires not only independent judicial oversight to protect fundamental liberties, but also a critical reevaluation of whether unrelenting pre-trial incarceration aligns with the enduring principles of justice and the rule of law.</p>
            <p>The tension between the CPC's foundational rights to liberty and SOSMA's uncompromising prioritisation of state security underscores a profound constitutional friction. As seen through recent case law and the resolute stance of the Malaysian Bar, defending the right to bail is crucial to ensure that pre-trial detention does not morph into arbitrary punishment. Preserving this balance requires not only active, independent judicial oversight to protect the fundamental rights guaranteed by the Federal Constitution, but also the critical role of Parliament in reforming legislation to restore and respect true judicial independence.</p>

